  ON THE WIRE, read with the DUT's own tcpdump (x960.md 7B.2):
    awplus# tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 4 -nn -v udp port 3784     [work/s7-bfdup-u2.log]
    05:34:23.074407 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, id 12857, flags [DF], proto UDP, length 52)
        10.0.61.1.49152 > 10.0.61.2.3784:
    05:34:23.314464 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, ... length 52)
        10.0.61.1.49152 > 10.0.61.2.3784:
    05:34:23.327195 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, ... length 52)
        10.0.61.2.49152 > 10.0.61.1.3784:
    05:34:23.567202 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, ... length 52)
        10.0.61.2.49152 > 10.0.61.1.3784:
    4 packets captured
    - bidirectional UDP/3784, TTL 255, TOS 0xc0 (CS6), 52-byte payload: BFD control.
    - spacing 240 ms in BOTH directions = 300 ms with RFC 5880 jitter (300 x 0.8).
    - this build's tcpdump mislabels the BFD payload "BCM-LI-SHIM ... (invalid)".
      The port, the TTL-255 and the length are what identify it; the decoder line
      is a dissector artefact and must not be read as a malformed packet.
  => C IS DISPROVEN: BFD is present, startable, configurable without any routing
     protocol, and genuinely running on the wire between these two devices.

LEG 5 -- THE DECISIVE MEASUREMENT: BFD DETECTS IN ~1.4 s, RIP IN ~171.6 s [done]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Stimulus: a SILENT NEIGHBOUR FAILURE -- `shutdown` on swi_b's vlan61 SVI. The
  physical port and the DUT's own vlan61 stay UP, so the DUT cannot learn of the
  failure from interface state; its only recourses are BFD and RIP's own timers.
  This is precisely the failure BFD exists for.

  Instrument: `tools/poll.py` on the DUT, 3 s interval, 250 s, sampling
  `show bfd peer brief` and `show ip route rip`, every sample epoch-stamped by
  the BOX.                                                  [work/s8-poll-u2.log]

    tools/tb poll tb277 ckt1406d u2 115200 3 250 \
        /tmp/ckt1406d/T47201/poll1.done "show bfd peer brief" "show ip route rip"

    event                                        box epoch        offset
    -------------------------------------------- ---------------  ---------
    `shutdown` echoed on swi_b                   1792128922.638   t0
    last DUT sample reading BFD **up**           1792128922.022   t0 - 0.6 s
    first DUT sample reading BFD **down**        1792128925.022   t0 + 2.4 s
    last sample with 192.168.70.0/24 in the FIB  1792129091.237   t0 + 168.6 s
    first sample with it GONE from the FIB       1792129094.238   t0 + 171.6 s

    BFD's own 1-second-resolution counter pins it finer. Read at 05:39:50 UTC
    (epoch 1792129190):                           [work/s9-after-timeout-u2.log]
      awplus# show bfd peer
          Status: down
          Downtime: 4 minute(s), 26 second(s)        -> transition at 1792128924
          Diagnostics: control detection time expired
    1792128924 - 1792128922.638 = **1.4 s**, consistent with the 900 ms detect
    time plus the time for `shutdown` to take effect on the far device. And the
    diagnostic says the session died of its OWN detect timer, not of an admin
    down -- so the 1.4 s is BFD detecting, not BFD being switched off.

    ** BFD: ~1.4 s.   RIP: ~171.6 s.   A factor of about 120. **
    (171.6 s is RIP's documented 180 s route timeout counted from the last update
    actually received, which arrived ~8 s before the shutdown.)

    After the timeout:                            [work/s9-after-timeout-u2.log]
      awplus# show ip route rip
      No entries in route table
      awplus# show ip rip database
      R  192.168.70.0/24  10.0.61.2  16  10.0.61.2  vlan61  00:18
      R  192.168.71.0/24  10.0.61.2  16  10.0.61.2  vlan61  00:18
    -- the FIB is clean and the RIP database holds both prefixes at metric 16 in
    the garbage-collect state, exactly the shape x960.md 7A.5 documents.

