================================================================================
FINDINGS
================================================================================
F-15 (new, the case's own result) -- THERE IS NO RIP-TO-BFD BINDING ON AW+,
  while BFD itself is fully functional on the same link between the same two
  devices. Proven by: the complete 24-command router-rip list with no `bfd`;
  `ip rip ?`'s six completions with no `bfd`; `bfd ?` -> `% Unrecognized command`
  in BOTH interface and router-rip mode; `neighbor <addr> ?` -> `<cr>` only; the
  SAME-interface contrast `ip ospf ?` -> `bfd  Bidirectional Forwarding
  Detection (BFD)`; and the product's own RIP Feature Overview Guide, which
  contains zero occurrences of "bfd" in 1045 lines. Measured with BOTH daemons
  running (bfdd pid 4391, ripd pid 4416) so it is neither the service gate nor a
  licence. Operational consequence, measured end to end: on a silent neighbour
  failure BFD knows in ~1.4 s and RIP still forwards into the black hole for
  ~171.6 s, with `show ip route` calling the dead prefix "best" the whole time.

O-31 -- `show bfd session` DOES NOT EXIST; the verb is `show bfd peer`
  (`brief`, `<addr> counters`, `vrf`). `show bfd session` answers `% Invalid
  input detected at '^' marker.`, which is one keystroke away from being read as
  "BFD is absent".

O-32 -- THE BFD REFUSAL HAS NO LICENCE CLAUSE, AND THAT IS INFORMATION.
  Before `service bfd`: `% BFD protocol daemon is not running`. Before
  `service rip`: `% RIP protocol daemon is not running or feature license is not
  available`. The missing half of the sentence is how you tell a service-gated-
  only feature from one that is also licence-gated, without running
  `show license`.

O-33 -- A BFD STATE CHANGE WRITES NOTHING TO THE DEVICE LOG.
  `show log | include bfd` and `| include BFD` across four measured up/down
  transitions returned only IMISH command echoes -- no state-change line at all.
  The ONLY record of when a BFD session failed is the `Uptime:` / `Downtime:`
  field of `show bfd peer`, which is 1-second resolution and is lost the moment
  the session re-establishes. Same family as O-15 (ping polling logs nothing).
  A deployment that wants an audit trail of BFD failures cannot get one here.

O-34 -- `no service bfd` SILENTLY DROPS YOU OUT OF CONFIGURATION MODE.
  Reproduced on both devices, twice each: the command is accepted, and the very
  next command (`end`) fails with `% Invalid input detected at '^' marker.`
  because the session is already at exec. The transcript shows the prompt going
  `awplus(config)#` -> `awplus#` with nothing sent in between
  [work/s20b-nosvcbfd-u2.log, work/s25-nosvc2-u2.log]. A stop-on-error config
  driver reads the failed `end` as a failure of the teardown and abandons
  whatever follows it -- so put `no service bfd` LAST in any batch. Same family
  as the documented `mls qos enable` / `atmf secure-mode` logout behaviour.

O-35 -- THIS BUILD'S `tcpdump` MISLABELS BFD CONTROL PACKETS as
  `BCM-LI-SHIM: direction unused, pkt-type unknown ... (invalid)`. The packets
  are correct BFD (UDP/3784, TTL 255, TOS 0xc0, 52-byte payload, bidirectional at
  the negotiated interval); only the dissector is wrong. Do not read "(invalid)"
  as a malformed packet.

O-36 -- `ip address <a>/<m> secondary` ON A LOOPBACK IS REFUSED WITH
  `% Configure primary first lo` IF `lo` HAS NO PRIMARY IPv4 ADDRESS, and the
  two units differed: the DUT's `lo` needed the address WITHOUT `secondary`,
  swi_b's needed it WITH. A recipe copied from one unit to the other stops at a
  `% ` line and cfg.py abandons the rest of the batch.

CONFIRMED, NOT NEW
  - x960.md 7A.1 service gate: `service <proto>` sent alone, ~10 s apart; no
    rejection on either device, either protocol.
  - x960.md 7A.2 / O-24: NO sticky `no service` line on a unit that did not BOOT
    with the service. Both devices came back to their exact pre-case `service`
    set with no reload. This is the first time the NEGATIVE half of O-24 has been
    checked deliberately, and it holds.
  - x960.md 7A.5: RIP defaults (send v2 only / receive v1 and v2, split horizon
    with poisoned reverse, AD 120, metric 2 one hop away, ~180 s timeout, the
    metric-16 garbage-collect state) and every v1-vs-v2 wire discriminator.
  - 7B.2: the DUT's own `tcpdump` is the instrument for a segment with no host
    NIC. It decoded RIP fully and BFD by port/size.
  - 7.10 / NEW-RISK-8: the `spanning-tree priority 0` + shut-the-spares remedy
    put port1.0.16 Designated Forwarding on the DUT and Rootport Forwarding on
    swi_b, confirmed on both devices BEFORE anything was measured.

================================================================================
WHY THE VERDICT IS UNSUPPORTED AND NOT FAIL
================================================================================
logged-output.md section 1: UNSUPPORTED is "the platform does not support what
the case tests"; FAIL is "it ran unsuccessfully: a check failed, or the platform
prevented it from running". Nothing here failed. BFD ran, RIP ran in both
versions, every counted leg behaved, every control was zero and every instrument
control answered. What the case asks for -- BFD *with* RIP -- is a capability the
platform does not have, and that was established with both daemons running, five
independent CLI probes, a same-interface A/B against OSPF, the vendor's own RIP
documentation, and an end-to-end dataplane measurement of the consequence. That
is a platform gap, measured, which is UNSUPPORTED.

VERDICT: UNSUPPORTED -- AW+ has no RIP-to-BFD binding on this platform (five CLI
probes + the RIP guide + an `ip ospf bfd` contrast on the same interface), while
BFD itself works: a `bfd peer` session came up over the same vlan61, ran on the
wire at 300 ms, and detected a silent neighbour failure in ~1.4 s (v2) and ~1.1 s
(v1) with `Diagnostics: control detection time expired` -- during which RIP kept
the dead prefix in the FIB and marked it "best" for ~171.6 s while the dataplane
measured 0/5 with its in-leg instrument control at 5/5.
